    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Intermediate First Year Physical Chemistry|States of matter and gas laws",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Gas laws describe relationships among pressure, volume, temperature and amount.\n\nA state of matter is a physical form determined by particle arrangement, motion and attraction.",
        "Boyle's law applies at constant temperature, Charles's law at constant pressure and Avogadro's law at fixed temperature and pressure. Temperature must be absolute.\n\nThe familiar states are solid, liquid and gas. Plasma occurs at very high energy, while a Bose-Einstein condensate forms near absolute zero.",
        "at constant temperature, compressing a gas from 2.0 L to 1.0 L doubles its pressure because P1V1 = P2V2.\n\nExample: ice, liquid water and water vapour are the same substance in three states; heating changes particle motion without changing H2O into a new substance.",
        "P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 for fixed amount\n\nheating or cooling <-> change in particle energy <-> change of state",
        "Never substitute Celsius temperature directly into gas-law ratios; convert it to kelvin.\n\nA state change does not automatically create a new substance.",
        "Gas laws guide tyres, syringes, weather balloons, breathing and gas storage.\n\nControlling states of matter is essential in refrigeration, cooking, metal casting and gas storage.",
        listOf("at constant temperature, compressing a gas from 2.0 L to 1.0 L doubles its pressure because P1V1 = P2V2.","Example: ice, liquid water and water vapour are the same substance in three states","heating changes particle motion without changing H2O into a new substance.","P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2 for fixed amount\n\nheating or cooling <-> change in particle energy <-> change of state","Boyle's law applies at constant temperature, Charles's law at constant pressure and Avogadro's law at fixed temperature and pressure.","Temperature must be absolute."),
        listOf("Boyle law","Charles law","Pressure","Kinetic particle theory","Phase changes","Temperature","Gas laws","States of matter")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Intermediate First Year Physical Chemistry|Thermodynamics at school level",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Internal energy is the total microscopic kinetic and potential energy of a system.\n\nEnthalpy is the state function H = U + PV, useful for constant-pressure processes.",
        "Its absolute value is usually unknown, but changes can be measured. The first law says energy changes through heat and work and is conserved overall.\n\nAt constant pressure with only pressure-volume work, the enthalpy change equals heat absorbed by the system. Negative deltaH is exothermic and positive deltaH is endothermic.",
        "if a system absorbs 300 J of heat and 80 J of work is done on it, deltaU = 300 + 80 = 380 J.\n\nExample: combustion releasing 890 kJ per mole has deltaH = -890 kJ mol-1; the negative sign records heat leaving the system.",
        "deltaU = q + w\n\nH = U + PV; at constant pressure deltaH = qp",
        "Do not omit the sign of work or assume internal energy depends only on temperature for every system.\n\nDo not drop the sign of deltaH or describe exothermic products as having more enthalpy than reactants.",
        "Internal-energy balances are used in reactors, engines, batteries and phase-change equipment.\n\nEnthalpy data support fuel comparison, process heating, food energy and reaction design.",
        listOf("if a system absorbs 300 J of heat and 80 J of work is done on it, deltaU = 300 + 80 = 380 J.","Example: combustion releasing 890 kJ per mole has deltaH = -890 kJ mol-1","the negative sign records heat leaving the system.","deltaU = q + w\n\nH = U + PV; at constant pressure deltaH = qp","Its absolute value is usually unknown, but changes can be measured.","The first law says energy changes through heat and work and is conserved overall."),
        listOf("First law","Heat","Work","State functions","Thermochemistry","Hess Law","Internal energy","Enthalpy")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Intermediate First Year Physical Chemistry|Chemical and ionic equilibrium",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "The equilibrium constant relates equilibrium activities or concentrations of products and reactants.\n\nIonic equilibrium describes reversible ionization and ion reactions in solution.",
        "Each term is raised to its stoichiometric coefficient; pure solids and liquids are omitted. K changes with temperature but not with initial composition.\n\nWeak acids, weak bases, water and sparingly soluble salts establish equilibria described by Ka, Kb, Kw and Ksp.",
        "for N2 + 3H2 <-> 2NH3, Kc = [NH3]^2/([N2][H2]^3). State the sign convention, substitute consistent units, and interpret the numerical result chemically.\n\nExample: for HA <-> H+ + A-, Ka = [H+][A-]/[HA]; a smaller Ka indicates less ionization under comparable conditions.",
        "Kc = product concentrations^coefficients / reactant concentrations^coefficients\n\nKw = [H3O+][OH-]; at 25 C, Kw = 1.0 x 10^-14",
        "Use equilibrium values, omit pure solids and liquids, and never include catalysts in the expression.\n\nDo not treat weak electrolytes as fully dissociated or ignore charge and mass balance.",
        "Equilibrium constants predict yield, dissociation, solubility and acid-base composition.\n\nIonic equilibria determine pH, precipitation, water chemistry and analytical separations.",
        listOf("for N2 + 3H2 <-> 2NH3, Kc = [NH3]^2/([N2][H2]^3).","State the sign convention, substitute consistent units, and interpret the numerical result chemically.","Example: for HA <-> H+ + A-, Ka = [H+][A-]/[HA]","a smaller Ka indicates less ionization under comparable conditions.","Kc = product concentrations^coefficients / reactant concentrations^coefficients\n\nKw = [H3O+][OH-]; at 25 C, Kw = 1.0 x 10^-14","Each term is raised to its stoichiometric coefficient"),
        listOf("Reaction quotient","Gibbs free energy","Le Chatelier principle","Acid dissociation","Solubility product","Common-ion effect","Equilibrium constant","Ionic equilibrium")),
