    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 11–12|Intermediate Second Year Inorganic and Physical|Coordination compounds exam basics",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Metal carbonyl bonding is synergic: carbon monoxide donates a lone pair to the metal while the metal back-donates d-electron density into CO antibonding orbitals.\n\nCrystal field theory models electrostatic splitting of metal d orbitals by surrounding ligands.",
        "Sigma donation forms M<-CO bonding and pi back-bonding strengthens M-C while weakening C-O. Greater back-donation lowers the C-O stretching frequency. Electron counting explains stable examples such as Ni(CO)4 and Fe(CO)5, while bridging and terminal carbonyls differ structurally and spectroscopically.\n\nModels electrostatic splitting of metal d orbitals by surrounding ligands.",
        "Ni in Ni(CO)4 has oxidation state zero. Nickel contributes 10 valence electrons and four neutral CO ligands donate 8, giving an 18-electron tetrahedral complex.\n\nIn an octahedral field, d orbitals split into lower t2g and higher eg sets separated by deltao.",
